# (subject, tag, my pick, key, mistake, fix, formula)   pick == key  ->  right but unsure
N=[]
def add(s,tag,pick,key,mistake,fix,formula=""): N.append((s,tag,pick,key,mistake,fix,formula))
# ---------------- Ethics
S='Ethics'
add(S,'Mock 6 Q43','A','C',"In a 'least likely violated' question I picked a Standard that WAS violated (Communication with Clients).","Mark every option violated / not violated first, then pick the one not violated. Here Fair Dealing was the one not breached; V(B) (fact vs opinion, disclose the process) and VI(B) (clients before own trades) were.","")
add(S,'Mock 8 Q39','C','A',"Treated 'disclose the finder's fee in the prospectus' as the wrong action.","VI(C) Referral Fees: disclose to employer, clients and prospects BEFORE the service, and the recommended update is at least QUARTERLY, not semiannually. Disclosure in the prospectus is fine.","")
add(S,'Mock 10 Q9','A','B',"Thought paid travel from a company I cover is acceptable when it does not change my decision.","I(B) Independence and Objectivity: always use commercial transport at your own or your firm's expense. A token gift is fine; paid travel is the violation (the appearance of compromise counts). Exception: remote site with no commercial transport.","")
# ---------------- FSA
S='FSA'
add(S,'Mock 6 Q23','C','B',"Picked 'low fluctuation of the cash-flow accruals ratio' as a sign of poor performance quality.","Red flags are: accruals ratio trending UP or erratic, and operating income consistently ABOVE cash from operations (Reason 3). Steady or falling accruals are not a concern.","Balance-sheet accruals = (NOA end − NOA begin) ÷ average NOA\nCash-flow accruals = (NI − CFO − CFI) ÷ average NOA")
add(S,'Mock 8 Q30','B','C',"Said a pension discount rate above peers is conservative.","Higher discount rate → lower PV of the obligation and lower expense → AGGRESSIVE (deficit understated). Lower rate = conservative.","PBO = Σ benefit_t ÷ (1 + discount rate)^t: rate ↑ → PBO ↓")
add(S,'Mock 8 Q31','A','A',"Right, but unsure why both quality labels apply.","Consistent losses on asset sales mean depreciation was understated in earlier years: operating income was overstated (low earnings quality) and the bias was deliberate and lagged (low financial reporting quality).","")
add(S,'Mock 8 Q32','C','B',"Said the debt-to-equity ratio decreases after cutting the pension discount rate by 0.5%.","Lower discount rate → larger obligation → liabilities ↑, equity ↓ (via AOCI), assets unchanged. So D/E ↑, financial leverage ↑ and book value per share ↓.","D/E = total debt ÷ equity: numerator ↑, denominator ↓ → ↑")
add(S,'Mock 9 Q25','C','A',"Linked Basel III to earnings quality.","Basel III raised the equity funding required against risk-weighted assets (limits leverage) and favours stable funding: consumer deposits are more stable than interbank. Earnings quality was not its target.","")
add(S,'Mock 9 Q26','C','A',"Expected underwriting standards to tighten after a hard market.","Hard market: high premiums and profits attract new competitors, the market softens, underwriting standards fall and capital is depleted (capital adequacy gets harder).","")
add(S,'Mock 9 Q28','B','C',"Looked only at the loss ratio below 100%.","Combined ratio adds the loss ratio and the underwriting expense ratio. Above 100% = underwriting loss that investment income must cover.","Combined ratio = (claims + loss adj. expenses) ÷ net premiums earned + underwriting expenses ÷ net premiums written")
add(S,'Mock 10 Q1','C','B',"Read the DuPont trend in the wrong direction (columns were newest year first).","Check the column order of the years before judging a trend. Rebuild assets and equity first, then compare leverage across years.","Total assets = revenue ÷ asset turnover\nEquity = assets − liabilities\nFinancial leverage = assets ÷ equity")
# ---------------- Equity
S='Equity'
add(S,'Mock 6 Q25','B','A',"Picked R65m: MVA must use the market value of the WHOLE firm (debt and equity) against total book capital.","MVA = market value of the firm − book value of total capital (market value of the firm includes the debt).","MVA = (MV debt + shares × price) − total book capital\n= 55 + 30 × 25.43 − 650 = R168m")
add(S,'Mock 6 Q26','A','B',"Said residual income is zero although intrinsic value (R22) is above book value (R20).","V0 > B0 → PV of future RI is positive; V0 = B0 → zero; V0 < B0 → negative.","V0 = B0 + PV(residual income)")
add(S,'Mock 7 Q12','B','C',"Multistage residual income value came out at €83.01 instead of €85.71 (the continuing value of RI was not built right).","Build RI year by year, then add the value of RI after the last year. Persistence factor 1 means RI stays constant forever.","RI_t = EPS_t − r × B_{t−1}\nV0 = B0 + Σ RI_t ÷ (1 + r)^t + PV(continuing RI)\nContinuing RI at T = RI_T × ω ÷ (1 + r − ω)")
add(S,'Mock 7 Q38','B','C',"Believed a high P/E is also justified by high risk.","High growth justifies a higher P/E, but higher risk raises the required return and justifies a LOWER P/E.","Justified P/E = payout ÷ (r − g): g ↑ → P/E ↑, r ↑ → P/E ↓")
add(S,'Mock 7 Q39','B','A',"Used the simple average (13.8) for an index P/E.","Index P/E uses the weighted HARMONIC mean (average the earnings yields). Order: harmonic < geometric < arithmetic.","Weighted harmonic = Σ weights ÷ Σ(weight ÷ P/E) = total market cap ÷ Σ(cap ÷ P/E) = 11.3")
add(S,'Mock 8 Q20','B','B',"Right, but unsure when the H-model stops working.","H-model fits a LINEAR fade from high to low growth; use a spreadsheet multistage model for a long high-growth period or a large gap between growth rates.","V0 = D0(1 + gL) ÷ (r − gL) + D0 × H × (gS − gL) ÷ (r − gL),   H = half-life = t ÷ 2")
add(S,'Mock 8 Q25','B','B',"Right, but unsure how to choose the model.","FCFF when assets are largely intangible and dividends are only occasional or special. DDM needs a consistent dividend; asset-based needs marketable assets.","")
add(S,'Mock 9 Q15','A','B',"Chose P/B for a company whose value is its brand.","P/B ignores internally built intangibles. High leverage and mixed capital structures → EV/sales. Dividend yield ignores capital gains.","EV = market cap + debt + preferred − cash")
# ---------------- Fixed Income
S='Fixed Income'
add(S,'Mock 6 Q40','B','C',"Said log-normality is not an input of tree calibration.","Calibration uses a volatility assumption, an interest rate model (log-normal) and the market values of the benchmark bonds. The bond being valued is NOT an input (it is valued afterwards on the tree).","")
add(S,'Mock 7 Q30','C','A',"Added the migration effect to the yield (sign flipped).","Expected return = YTM + expected % price change. A spread widening lowers the price.","E(R) = YTM + Σ prob_i × [−MD × (spread_i − spread_0)]\n= 5.50% − 1.77% = 3.73%")
add(S,'Mock 7 Q31','B','C',"Got 0.9388% instead of 1.4082%: one step of the CVA chain was off.","Always do three steps: VND from the tree, then CVA, then the yield of (VND − CVA) minus the benchmark yield.","CVA = Σ DF(benchmark) × PD × LGD × expected exposure\nFair value = VND − CVA\nSpread = YTM(fair value) − benchmark YTM")
add(S,'Mock 8 Q10','A','B',"Used the wrong loss given default (0.7796% is what LGD 50% gives).","Recovery of one-quarter means LGD = 75%. Convert recovery to LGD before anything else.","LGD = 1 − recovery rate\nPD_t = h × (1 − h)^(t−1)\nCVA = Σ DF × PD × LGD × exposure")
add(S,'Mock 8 Q11','C','C',"Right, but unsure of the direction.","Credit spread falls when the risk-neutral PD falls (CVA ↓ → fair value ↑ → yield ↓). A higher PD or higher LGD widens the spread.","")
add(S,'Mock 9 Q6','B','C',"Confused liquidity preference with pure expectations.","Pure expectations: forward rates are unbiased predictors of future spot rates (the future curve = today's forward curve). Liquidity preference: forward = expected spot + liquidity premium. Segmented markets: each maturity has its own supply and demand.","")
add(S,'Mock 9 Q8','A','A',"Right, but unsure how the two views combine.","Flight to quality → yields fall → extend duration. Bullish flattening (long rates fall more than short) → barbell rather than bullet.","")
# ---------------- Derivatives
S='Derivatives'
add(S,'Mock 7 Q23','C','B',"Took the fixed rate of the wrong currency (the Korean rate, which the Foundation pays).","Decide which leg is received and which is paid by currency first. Compute each fixed rate from its own country's discount factors.","Swap fixed rate = (1 − PV_n) ÷ Σ PV_i  (that country's factors)\n= (1 − 0.9260) ÷ 4.7995 = 1.54%")
add(S,'Mock 8 Q1','C','B',"Left the bond cost uncompounded in the bond futures arbitrage.","Carry the bond price plus accrued interest forward to expiry. Futures side = CF × futures price + accrued interest at expiry. Discount only the final difference.","F0 = (1 + r)^T × (B0 + AI0)\nImplied = CF × Q0 + AI_T\nProfit = (implied − F0) ÷ (1 + r)^T")
add(S,'Mock 10 Q23','B','B',"Right, but unsure which Greek to use.","'All else equal, one day later' means only time decay: value falls by theta.","New value = old value + theta × days  (theta is negative)\nFull: ΔC ≈ Δ·ΔS + ½Γ·ΔS² + Θ·Δt")
add(S,'Mock 10 Q24','A','B',"Gave the European put value (8.45); forgot early exercise at the down node.","American option: at EVERY node take the larger of the binomial value and the exercise value. Down node: 20 > 16.77, so use 20.","π = (1 + r − d) ÷ (u − d)\nV = [π V_u + (1 − π) V_d] ÷ (1 + r)\nAmerican: V = max(V, K − S)")
add(S,'Mock 6 Q36','A','B',"Accepted Jeffries' claim that the smile and the skew take the same shape.","Implied volatility is backed out of market option prices and is forward-looking. The volatility surface shows IV across strikes AND maturities. Smile and skew do not have the same shape (hedging demand steepens the skew).","")
# ---------------- Quant
S='Quant'
add(S,'Mock 7 Q33','B','A',"Called the variable significant although |t| was below the critical value.","Compare |t| with the critical value for n − k − 1 degrees of freedom: STIM |−2.10| < 2.12 (not significant), CRISIS 2.35 > 2.12 (significant).","t = coefficient ÷ standard error")
add(S,'Mock 8 Q15','C','C',"Right, but unsure how ARCH is tested.","Regress SQUARED residuals on their own lag: t above the critical value → ARCH. Residuals on their own lag test serial correlation.","ε²_t = a0 + a1 ε²_{t−1}")
add(S,'Mock 9 Q34','C','A',"Accepted that SEE measures errors of an independent variable.","SEE is the standard deviation of the differences between actual and estimated values of the DEPENDENT variable. Small SEE means strong fit.","SEE = √[SSE ÷ (n − k − 1)]")
add(S,'Mock 9 Q36','B','B',"Right, but unsure which test applies.","One slope coefficient: t-test. All slopes together: F-test. R² and SEE describe the fit, not a single coefficient.","")
add(S,'Mock 10 Q13','C','A',"Expected the slope of a random walk to be near 0.","Random walk as an AR(1): b0 ≈ 0 and b1 ≈ 1 (unit root).","x_t = b0 + b1 x_{t−1} + ε:  b0 ≈ 0, b1 ≈ 1")
add(S,'Mock 10 Q14','A','A',"Right, but unsure.","For a random walk the best forecast of the next value is the CURRENT value (no mean reversion, not covariance stationary).","")
add(S,'Mock 10 Q15','C','C',"Right, but unsure.","ARCH(1) is present when the slope c1 on the lagged squared residual is significantly different from 0.","")
# ---------------- Portfolio Management
S='Portfolio Management'
add(S,'Mock 6 Q31','A','B',"Called filling a temporary benchmark gap 'rebalancing'.","ETF uses: completion = fill a gap in benchmark exposure; rebalancing = return to target weights after market moves; liquidity management = equitize cash for inflows and outflows.","")
add(S,'Mock 7 Q41','C','A',"Picked 6.00% instead of the model intercept (2.58%).","In a macro factor model the factors are SURPRISES; expected return is the intercept when all surprises are zero.","R_i = a_i + b_i1 F1 + b_i2 F2 + … + ε;   E(R_i) = a_i")
add(S,'Mock 7 Q43','A','C',"Judged one statement wrong although both were true.","Macro model: factors = surprises in economic variables, sensitivities estimated LAST. Fundamental model: factors = company attributes, sensitivities specified FIRST.","")
add(S,'Mock 7 Q44','A','C',"Agreed that multifactor models are useless for passive management.","Multifactor models serve BOTH active management (tilts, risk) and passive management (replicate an index's factor exposures).","")
add(S,'Mock 9 Q41','B','C',"Called VaR 'subjective' as an advantage.","VaR advantages: basis for risk-adjusted performance evaluation, comparable across portfolios. Its limits: not a worst case, subjective inputs.","")
add(S,'Mock 9 Q43','A','B',"Described historical simulation, not the parametric method.","Parametric VaR assumes normally distributed returns: poor for fat tails and options. Historical uses past data; Monte Carlo needs many random scenarios.","VaR = (μ − z × σ) × portfolio value")
# ---------------- Economics
S='Economics'
add(S,'Mock 6 Q19','A','C',"Counted a low exchange rate as a growth channel on the supply side.","Capital deepening and investment in technology raise potential growth. A low currency supports export demand and has little direct effect on supply capacity.","")
add(S,'Mock 7 Q20','B','C',"Chose a central bank action that expands money when inflation is the threat.","Inflation threat → contract money: sell domestic securities (open market), raise rates.","")
add(S,'Mock 8 Q6','A','C',"Accepted a false inflation-linked bond statement.","Break-even inflation = nominal yield − real yield (same maturity). More inflation uncertainty → HIGHER break-even (inflation risk premium). It does not compare long and short linkers.","BEI = y(nominal) − y(inflation-linked)")
add(S,'Mock 8 Q8','C','B',"Misread credit spreads over the business cycle.","In a downturn spreads widen and high-rated bonds outperform. When spreads narrow, weaker credits improve MORE.","")
# ---------------- Corporate Issuers
S='Corporate Issuers'
add(S,'Mock 6 Q16','B','A',"Said ESG integration lowers the credit spread for a company with poor governance.","Few independent directors, weak diversity and high social risk → higher risk perception → credit spread INCREASES.","")
add(S,'Mock 8 Q34','C','A',"Compared the companies without scoring each feature.","Score every feature separately (1 point per good-governance feature), then add: here the totals were the same.","")
add(S,'Mock 9 Q9','B','A',"In an 'INCORRECT statement' question I picked a correct statement.","Retiring debt improves the rating and LOWERS the cost of capital. Sale-leasebacks unlock real estate value. Re-read whether the question asks for correct or incorrect.","")
add(S,'Mock 9 Q12','A','A',"Right, but unsure.","Spin-off: shares of the subsidiary go to existing shareholders, no cash. Equity carve-out: sells shares to new investors, cash to the parent. Divestiture: sells the segment.","")
# ---------------- Alternative Investments
S='Alternative Investments'
add(S,'Mock 6 Q2','C','A',"Thought a REIT needs less expertise than a REOC.","REIT is a pass-through (no corporate tax, no double taxation). A REOC is taxed as a company and can hold properties under development.","")
add(S,'Mock 8 Q43','B','B',"Right, but unsure how the merger arbitrage gain works.","Long target, short acquirer. If the deal closes, the target shares convert into acquirer shares that cover the short.","Gain = short proceeds − cost of the target shares\n= 350,000 − 330,000 = $20,000")
add(S,'Mock 8 Q44','B','B',"Right, but unsure of the main risk.","The main risk is deal failure: the target falls back to its pre-announcement price while the short position no longer offsets it.","")
add(S,'Mock 9 Q40','B','A',"Matched obsolescence of space to a storage REIT.","Storage REIT → new competing facilities (easy entry). Office REIT → obsolescence. Retail REIT → tenants' sales per square foot.","")
add(S,'Mock 10 Q37','A','B',"Compared dollar amounts of unreimbursed expenses.","Compare unreimbursed expenses as a % of effective gross income: Boston 44.5% vs New York 36.7%, so Boston is hit harder.","Unreimbursed expenses % = (operating expenses − reimbursements) ÷ EGI")
add(S,'Mock 10 Q38','A','C',"Thought both statements could describe the same cycle phase.","High vacancy = oversupply or recession phase, so only a recovery within two years (Statement 2) fits. The two statements imply different phases.","")
add(S,'Mock 10 Q40','A','A',"Right, but unsure.","Direct capitalisation uses one period's NOI. If NOI is temporarily disrupted, use a NORMALIZED NOI. Sales comparison is less suited while a recovery is expected.","Value = NOI ÷ cap rate")
